CHECK bb22.1 TRUE n=1 is an error ("at least two rows"); n in {2,3,4,5} fits only the intercept (no forward step, termcond 2) under default AND minspan=1,endspan=1 alike; n in {8,12} adds forward terms
CHECK bb22.2a TRUE a constant y fits (rss=gcv=0, termcond 4, an RSq-change stop) rather than erroring, whether or not x has a noise-free deterministic structure, and WARNS instead of erroring
CHECK bb22.2b TRUE y with all but one value equal: the forward pass records candidate hinges, but pruning selects the intercept alone (1 selected term)
CHECK bb22.3a TRUE a constant column next to a useful one is never used (no dirs row names it)
CHECK bb22.3b TRUE an exactly duplicated column resolves to the LOWER column index only (x2 never appears in any term)
CHECK bb22.3c FALSE HYPOTHESIS a near-duplicate (not exact) column is resolved the same way, to one column only (it is not: the unpruned terms use x1 (TRUE) and use x2 (TRUE), both present)
CHECK bb22.3d TRUE instead, a near-duplicate is treated as a genuinely different (not tied) column at every knot search, so different terms of the same fit can use either near-duplicate column, and the fit does not match dropping the near-duplicate column outright (rss 0.4767 vs 0.4397)
CHECK bb22.4 TRUE n < p (n=20,p=50) and p=200,n=300 fit without error, and the default nk follows min(200,max(20,2p))+1 in both; p=1 also fits normally
CHECK bb22.5 TRUE a single extreme outlier value (1e6 among [0,1]) does not cause an error; the row is kept and fit like any other point
CHECK bb22.6a TRUE NaN or Inf anywhere in x or y is always a fitting error (never silently dropped or coerced); for x, and for a plain NaN in y, the message names the true offending row (5)
CHECK bb22.6b FALSE HYPOTHESIS an Inf in y also names row 5, the row that actually holds the Inf, the way Inf in x and NaN in y do
CHECK bb22.6c TRUE instead, an Inf anywhere in y makes mean(y) and sd(y) non-finite (shown above), so scaling turns EVERY element to NaN and the error always names row 1, regardless of where the Inf actually was
